@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-460  Euro pricing goes live Monday. Double the currency test
             table, retire the deprecated provider methods, and archive
             the sprint notes.

Four files are open. Work through them in order, `:w` before each
`:bn`, and finish with `:wqa`.

  1. currency.spec.ts — the refund window must not depend on currency,
     so every `'GBP'` row in the test table needs a twin directly below
     it that is identical except for `'EUR'`. The `USD` rows are not
     touched, and neither is the assertion further down that also
     says `'GBP'`.

  2. order.provider.ts — three methods are marked `@deprecated`. Each
     goes, comment and all, leaving one blank line between what remains.

  3. sprint-notes.md — the sprint is closing. Copy every open `- [ ]`
     item to the bottom under `## Carry over`, in order, then delete
     every ticked `- [x]` item from the daily sections.

  4. errors.json — open for reference. Leave it alone.

Nothing here needs a key you have not used in this chapter. The
difficulty is that each file needs a different one of them, and the
first file needs a pattern more careful than the obvious one.

@keys
| `:g/pattern/t.|s/old/new/` | copy each match below itself, then edit the copy |
| `:g/pattern/normal dap` | delete a paragraph at every match |
| `:g/pattern/t$` | collect a copy of every match at the end |
| `:g/pattern/d` | delete every match |
| `:w` `:bn` `:wqa` | write, next buffer, write all and quit |

@hints
In the spec, `GBP` on its own marks the assertion line too, and so does
`'GBP'`, and so does `, 'GBP'`. Look at what the table rows have that
the assertion does not, at the start of the line rather than the end.
--
The rows begin with an opening square bracket after the indentation.
`^\s*\[.*GBP` says "a table row that mentions GBP" and nothing else
in the file matches it.
--
The provider is lesson 10 exactly. The notes are lesson 13 followed by
a `:g//d`; do the copy first, so the ticked items are gone from the
sections but the open ones still exist to be copied.
--
`:g/^\s*\[.*GBP/t.|s/GBP/EUR/`, `:w`, `:bn`, `:g/@deprecated/normal dap`,
`:w`, `:bn`, `:g/^- \[ \]/t$`, `:g/\[x\]/d`, `:wqa`.

@solution
**currency.spec.ts** — `:g/^\s*\[.*GBP/t.|s/GBP/EUR/`. The opening
bracket at the start of the line is what a table row has and the
`expect` line does not; that is the whole reason this file is first.
`:w`, `:bn`.

**order.provider.ts** — `:g/@deprecated/normal dap`. `:w`, `:bn`.

**sprint-notes.md** — `:g/^- \[ \]/t$` to collect, then `:g/\[x\]/d` to
sweep. `:wqa`.

**errors.json** — nothing.

Five commands across three files. If your count is far above par, the
likely reason is that one of the files was done a line at a time; go
back and find the pattern that names all of its lines.

The pattern in the spec is the lesson inside the scenario. `'GBP'`
appears on six lines and only five are rows; the sixth is an assertion
whose string happens to contain it, and it is written so that the
tempting refinements, the quotes, the comma, all still match it. Every
`:g` starts with the question from lesson 2: what do the lines I want
have that the lines I do not want lack? Here it was the bracket at the
left edge. It is usually something that small, and finding it is faster
than checking the result by eye afterwards.

The order in the notes file matters, and it is a general habit: when
one `:g` deletes and another copies, run the copy first. Deleting first
can only lose you material.

@start currency.spec.ts
import { isRefundable } from './refund-window';
import { withDefaults } from './order.defaults';

describe('isRefundable', () => {
  const cases = [
    ['ord_4001', 'GBP', 4999, 3, true],
    ['ord_4002', 'GBP', 12000, 29, true],
    ['ord_4003', 'USD', 4999, 3, true],
    ['ord_4004', 'GBP', 5, 30, true],
    ['ord_4005', 'USD', 100, 31, false],
    ['ord_4006', 'GBP', 100000, 31, false],
    ['ord_4007', 'GBP', 0, 90, false],
  ];

  it.each(cases)('%s in %s, total %i, %i days old', (id, currency, total, ageInDays, expected) => {
    expect(isRefundable({ id, currency, total, ageInDays })).toBe(expected);
  });

  it('defaults the currency to sterling', () => {
    expect(withDefaults({ id: 'ord_4008', total: 250 }, 'GBP').currency).toBe('GBP');
  });
});

@expected currency.spec.ts
import { isRefundable } from './refund-window';
import { withDefaults } from './order.defaults';

describe('isRefundable', () => {
  const cases = [
    ['ord_4001', 'GBP', 4999, 3, true],
    ['ord_4001', 'EUR', 4999, 3, true],
    ['ord_4002', 'GBP', 12000, 29, true],
    ['ord_4002', 'EUR', 12000, 29, true],
    ['ord_4003', 'USD', 4999, 3, true],
    ['ord_4004', 'GBP', 5, 30, true],
    ['ord_4004', 'EUR', 5, 30, true],
    ['ord_4005', 'USD', 100, 31, false],
    ['ord_4006', 'GBP', 100000, 31, false],
    ['ord_4006', 'EUR', 100000, 31, false],
    ['ord_4007', 'GBP', 0, 90, false],
    ['ord_4007', 'EUR', 0, 90, false],
  ];

  it.each(cases)('%s in %s, total %i, %i days old', (id, currency, total, ageInDays, expected) => {
    expect(isRefundable({ id, currency, total, ageInDays })).toBe(expected);
  });

  it('defaults the currency to sterling', () => {
    expect(withDefaults({ id: 'ord_4008', total: 250 }, 'GBP').currency).toBe('GBP');
  });
});

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  /** @deprecated use findOne */
  public async get(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });
  }

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  /** @deprecated totals are stored in pence now */
  public async totalInPounds(id: string) {
    return (await this.findOne(id)).total / 100;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  /** @deprecated the shipping consumer owns this transition */
  public async markShipped(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.update({ where: { id }, values: { status: 'SHIPPED' } });
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });
  }
}

